[Music]  another question here from jennifer  how much of the tv show hoarders is  actually real versus a staged  exaggeration and what does the  professional organizer community think  about these shows well ryan and i were  just at the emmys and they sat us at the  table with one of the hosts of hoarders  which somebody planned  um and his wife uh she was lovely and  they  were talking to us about hoarding  and the show hoarders and even he he was  like i was seeing my own hoarding  through these people but it was easy for  me to  laugh and ridicule the stage five orders  because they have  dead pets in the freezer or feces on the  floor in the bathroom or they have  blocked exits and at least i'm not that  bad right  we were on rachel cruz's show recently  let's put a link to that in the show  notes we did like a 20 30 minute tv show  spot with her we talked about the five  stages of hoarding and the truth is the  first stage of hoarding most people  are stage one hoarders right light  clutter in more than one room and you're  a stage one hoarder yeah and no  noticeable odors  well you must be stage two then  wait a minute  so julia we were talking to  matt about  boarding  and about the tv show hoarders and it is  a accurate representation of these  people's lives now here's the thing  these are  extreme cases obviously it's we don't  feature stage one hoarders on the tv  show hoarders because like that's  everyone right what  we need to sensationalize things so we  need to see stage five we need to see  the blocked exits we need to see the  tiny pathways so you can barely walk  through someone's home  and so yes it is sensationalized for tv  and edited in a way that makes it more  compelling because let's face it we've  also driven by a hoarder's yard a  hoarder's house i'm not compelled in the  sense that i want to watch their yard  for 30 minutes so of course a tv show  has to make it more compelling but you  work in people's houses all the time who  are hoarders they may not be stage five  all the time but you're seeing stage two  three four hoarders  and it's very real  it's absolutely real yeah yeah i mean  i've seen the show hoarders as well um  and i recently read matt paxton's book  about legacy list and letting go and i  found it helpful in thinking about you  know later in life the things that i  wanted to be remembered for in terms of  material possessions uh yes i see  hoarded homes regularly  the home that i was speaking about  earlier with all of the paper covering  the desk was an example of that and i  think a lot of people that i work with  are not they're aware that there's a  problem but they're they don't see  things the same way that an outsider  would see it because they've got so used  to it yeah and they do a lot of just  stuff shifting they're just turning  things around you know they'll do  they'll just move things from one room  to the next um what i consider what i do  is somewhere between what you see on the  home edit and what you see on hoarders  it's very much in the middle  one thing that i find so fascinating is  that when i work with people in  hollywood which i do sometimes because  i'm located in la they don't allow me to  take pictures of their home or videos of  their home so  what we see on the home that it is these  celebrities super excited to come in but  we are not seeing a celebrities who are  in hoarded homes because of the shame  that they have i see these people sadly  they don't let me take a selfie like you  know with them because they don't want  they don't want anyone to know that it  was like that but um whether you're a  celebrity or not um this is a very real  problem that i see all the time and i  see it in homes that you don't see in  movies so you know some in a lot of  movies you see like the really wealthy  home with the whole staff working in  there and it looks pristine and  immaculate i see those homes in santa  monica and beverly hills and brentwood  that are also hoarded so i see  like i said it's somewhere between where  i exist is between the two so i would  say there is reality very much so to the  show hoarders but as you said it is  sensationalized  yeah it's almost like like so these  homes you're talking about it's like  these beautiful pieces of art really  uh-huh but then when you cram it full of  stuff it takes that beauty away from it  it's like throwing a cake at mona lisa  yeah right yeah  yeah  yeah too soon josh yeah and i i mean i'm  really just there to support them they  already feel pretty terrible about the  state of things and um you know i often  wonder at the show hoarders how they get  people to agree to be on the show  because many of the people i work with  really strongly do not want any evidence  of anyone knowing that it was even close  to where they live where we were yeah i  feel like the hoarders episodes are like  people who are just like completely  desperate like they're just so desperate  they're like fine let's film it help me  get rid of stuff what's fascinating to  me and i'm kind of going off the rail  here a little bit what's fascinating to  me is that show a lot of the times  has instances where the home isn't safe  so there's so much stuff  and there's so much weight that it  structurally changes the house  and then they always are like hey we  your house is unsafe to live in like you  need to move out and so they'll move out  but they always end up moving back in  like always just yeah so like for the  show they move out and then there's like  a follow-up and it's like oh yeah  six months later they just moved right  back into the house and nothing really  changed yeah and the reason nothing  changes is it don't get to  the why  if you don't understand why  you want to let go or why you're holding  on so tightly  boarding is part of the ocd spectrum  it's the same thing as spartanism but  just the other side of the spectrum  spartans can't hold on to anything  they keep letting go unintentionally  slipping off right it's like they have  butterfingers and they can't hold on to  anything relationships stuff careers  cities etc  orders can't let go of anything and so  they cling  tightly  and the only reason we cling tightly is  because we don't see the benefits of  letting go  i can briefly share a personal story of  how i relate to this so i'm i never  identified as a hoarder at all however a  lot of my life experience has very much  have informed the way that i approach  organizing and as i was mentioning  earlier with finding a therapist you  have to ultimately be the one asking for  help so  i have been in a rehab setting in my  past and when i saw people who came in  there who were there by intervention by  their families by court order who had to  be there for their drug or alcohol  addiction those people were much less  likely to get well to get better than  people who were there on their own  accord yeah and so um even if it isn't  hoarding i can relate to that because i  have seeked help in the past i had an  eating disorder and i went to seek  treatment myself and ultimately i  believe that's why i got better it  wasn't because somebody else asked me to  do it  it was because i was ready and i was  ready to get help at that time but the  other people that were there again  through intervention not so much and so  with that show hoarders i love that the  show exists because if it motivates  people to get help that's fantastic i'm  sure some people do watch it because  they just want to feel better about  themselves i mean i think a lot of us  watch tv to unwind and like look at how  much of a hot mess that house is or like  any reality show look how much better i  am but if it leads people to seek help  that's fantastic and i'm glad it exists  yeah that's a yeah that's a great point  like you can't help someone who doesn't  want to be helped  that's absolutely true did you enjoy  this standalone patreon highlight if so  you can l